          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 52 and my father had a heart attack at 55.",
          "answer": "**Seek emergency care now.** What you have described can be a medical emergency. Call emergency services or go to an emergency department immediately. Do not wait to see whether it improves, and do not drive yourself.\n\nA family history of early heart disease can increase an individual's personal risk for coronary artery disease. Your father having a heart attack at age 55 would be considered a family history of premature coronary disease, which is a factor that contributes to this risk (condition-coronary-artery-disease). This family history is also considered a risk-enhancing feature that clinicians consider when assessing overall cardiovascular risk (guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation).\n\nGeneral preventative measures often focus on modifying factors that contribute to coronary artery disease. These include:\n*   Avoiding smoking.\n*   Managing high blood pressure and raised LDL cholesterol.\n*   Controlling diabetes.\n*   Addressing obesity.\n*   Increasing physical activity.\n*   Adopting a diet low in saturated fat and rich in vegetables and fiber (condition-coronary-artery-disease).\n\nYou might discuss these points and your family history with a clinician to understand your personal risk and the most appropriate preventative strategies for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
