          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does that change what I should be screened for?",
          "answer": "Yes, your family history of a father having a heart attack at 55 is considered a risk-enhancing feature that clinicians consider when assessing your overall cardiovascular risk (guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation). This can alter recommended screening practices.\n\nFor lipid screening, the usual interval is every four to six years for adults aged 20 to 75 who are at low risk. However, the interval is shorter when risk factors are present or when risk is being reassessed before a treatment decision (guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation). Your family history of premature coronary disease would be a risk factor to consider.\n\nRegarding type 2 diabetes screening, guidance recommends screening adults from age 35, and also screening younger adults who are overweight or obese and have at least one additional risk factor. While your age already puts you in the general screening group, having established cardiovascular disease in a first-degree relative is a risk factor that could prompt earlier or more frequent screening if you had other risk factors like being overweight or obese (guideline-type-2-diabetes-screening).\n\nIt's important to discuss your family history and other personal risk factors with a clinician. They can help determine your estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk, which drives decisions about screening frequency and potential interventions (guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.\n[sources: guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation, guideline-type-2-diabetes-screening]",
